{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's 'fictionalism' defense fails because it mischaracterizes Popper's actual solution. Popper did not advocate acting 'as if' theories are probable (a pragmatic fiction); he argued that preferring better-corroborated theories is rational because corroboration reports on a theory's testability and severity of tests passed, which are objective logical relations, not subjective probabilities or methodological fictions. By reducing the preference to a 'necessary rule of the game' or 'fiction,' the target ignores Popper's specific distinction between probability (inductive) and verisimilitude/testability (deductive/logical), thereby failing to defend the preference on 'Popper's own terms' as required by the problem.","premise":"Popper explicitly rejected instrumentalism/fictionalism in favor of realism and defined corroboration as a logical report on past performance relative to testability, distinct from both probability and pragmatic utility.","successor_question":"If corroboration is merely a fictional rule rather than a report on objective testability, how does Popper's system avoid collapsing into the very conventionalism he criticized in 'The Logic of Scientific Discovery'?","target":"4c795ca1879e091babeec0a5009da08dbcf28ec7ff04fe9bd7274c342f663805"}]}